  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT SAT OCT 17 2026\n\nThis evening a large ridge stretches across much of Conus from\nNew England to the Great Lakes to Texas and the Deep South. A\n1035 mb center is now over Ohio. Surface pressure gradients are\nbeginning to relax as ridging settles across the region. Winds\nare responding to this change from yesterday and beginning to\ntaper down tonight. Winds should start to become NE to E on Sunday\nand not quite as gusty in response to placement adjustments of \nthe surface ridge. Cool fall readings are on tap with lows in the \n40s for most of this week. We could even see an isolated low or \ntwo in the upper 30s in a few of the colder northern locales and \nvalleys for Sunday and Monday nights. Highs will be generally in \nthe 60s for Sunday with a few lower 70s. While overall Eastern \nConus ridging continues this week, it does weaken a little. As \nsuch, our airmass should begin to moderate as we head through the \nupcoming week. Look for highs by Monday to be seasonal in the 70s,\nwith lower 80s creeping back for some by Wednesday. However, we \nwill remain rainfree with very little moisture available for \ncloud cover, much less rain.\n\n08",
